
Goal:
The password for the next level is stored in the file data.txt 
and is the only line of text that occurs only once.

Approach:
This challenge is a "find the unique line" problem in a file full 
of duplicate lines. Two core Linux utilities solve it:

 sort   -> arranges lines alphabetically, so identical lines end up 
            next to each other (required before using uniq).
 uniq -u -> prints only lines that have no adjacent duplicates, 
             i.e., lines that occur exactly once.

Command:
sort data.txt | uniq -u

Breakdown:
1. sort data.txt   -> sorts all lines so duplicates become adjacent.
2. | uniq -u        -> filters and prints only the line with no 
                        duplicates (the unique line = password).

Result:
The output is a single line, which is the password for level 9.

Key Takeaway:
uniq only detects duplicates that are adjacent to each other, so 
sorting first is mandatory. The combo "sort | uniq -u" is a common 
pattern for finding unique entries in logs, wordlists, or datasets.
